@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+# Aves Custom build and update notes
+
+This working tree is based on Aves `1.15.10+184` / Flutter `3.47.8`. The personal custom build uses the `izzy` product flavor with application ID `deckers.thibault.aves.custom`, so it installs alongside the official Aves app.
+
+## Important installation/signing warning
+
+The official Aves release is signed with the upstream developer's private signing key. This project does not contain that key. Android requires both the application ID and signing certificate to match for an in-place update. This build deliberately uses a different application ID (`deckers.thibault.aves.custom`), so the official app remains installed and untouched. The custom build can then be updated over itself as long as every future build uses the same private keystore and alias.
+
+**Do not lose the custom keystore.** If it is lost, Android will not accept future APKs as updates to this custom installation. Never commit the keystore or `android/key.properties` to GitHub.
+
+## Build prerequisites
+
+- Flutter SDK `3.47.8`
+- Build variant: `izzyRelease` (without the upstream Play/Firebase registration)
+- Android SDK with platform 37 and the build tools/NDK required by the Flutter/Gradle setup
+- Java 21 (Temurin/Adoptium is the configured toolchain vendor)
+- Network access for `flutter pub get`, Maven/Gradle and Git-based Dart dependencies
+- A persistent release keystore
+- An optional Google Maps API key if Google-backed map functionality is required; without it the Gradle config uses the `NONE` fallback
+
+## Configure signing
+
+1. Use the separately supplied `aves-custom-release.jks` signing key. Copy it to `android/aves-custom-release.jks`.
+2. Copy `android/key.properties.example` to `android/key.properties`.
+3. Set `storeFile=../aves-custom-release.jks`, `keyAlias=aves-custom`, and enter the supplied store/key password in both password fields. The path is resolved from the Android app Gradle project directory.
+4. Keep at least two secure backups of the keystore and its credentials, stored separately. The password is not included in the source patch.
+
+If you choose to generate a different key instead, do it only before the first custom installation. For example:
+
+```bash
+keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore android/aves-custom-release.jks -storetype JKS -storepass CHANGE_ME -keypass CHANGE_ME -alias aves-custom -keyalg RSA -keysize 4096 -validity 10000 -dname "CN=Aves Custom Release"
+```
+
+## Build
+
+From the project root run:
+
+```bash
+./scripts/build_custom_apk.sh
+```
+
+The script runs `flutter analyze`, `flutter test`, then builds the `izzyRelease` APK. The expected output is `build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-izzy-release.apk`.
+
+## Updating from a future Aves source snapshot
+
+This source patch is for the supplied `1.15.10+184` tree. For a later upstream version:
+
+1. Start from a clean copy of the new upstream source.
+2. Apply `aves_custom.patch` with `git apply --check aves_custom.patch`, then `git apply aves_custom.patch`.
+3. Resolve any reported conflicts by reviewing the new upstream implementation; do not force-apply blindly.
+4. Increment the `+buildNumber` in `pubspec.yaml` above the version currently installed on the device.
+5. Run the full analyze/test/build script and verify the APK signature is the same key as previous custom releases.
+
+A source patch is not a binary patch and cannot safely be applied to an arbitrary APK. Every release must be rebuilt from source.
+
+## Verification status for this handoff
+
+The source changes were reviewed statically and JSON/l10n files were syntax-checked. `flutter analyze`, `flutter test`, Gradle build, Android install and runtime testing have **not** been run in the current workspace because Flutter/Dart and Android SDK are not installed here and the environment cannot reach SDK distribution hosts. Do not treat this source patch as a tested APK release until those checks pass.
